@page "/attach"

<PageTitle>Attaching to your own EditForm</PageTitle>
<h1>Attaching to your own EditForm</h1>
<p>In attach mode, Formidable works inside a form the page already owns: this page keeps its own <code>EditForm</code> and adds a <code>FormidableValidator</code> to it. Removing an expense line takes its error off the page, with nothing extra asked of the code that removed it.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "ExpenseReport.cs", "AttachMode.razor", "AttachMode.razor.cs" })">
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Click <strong>Submit</strong> without changing anything.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> "Description is required" under the second, empty line and in the error summary, focus in that line, and no error on <strong>Submitted by</strong>.</div></li>
            <li>Click <strong>Remove</strong> on that line.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> the line goes, and its entry leaves the error summary, with no second submit.</div><div class="teaching__why"><span class="teaching__label">Why:</span> <code>FormidableValidator</code> notices the line's input leave and re-checks the form unasked.</div></li>
            <li>Click <strong>Add expense</strong>, then click <strong>Submit</strong> with the new line empty.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> the new line gets "Description is required" and an entry in the error summary.</div></li>
            <li>Click <strong>Remove</strong> on the new line.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> the line goes, and its entry leaves the error summary, as in step 2.</div></li>
            <li>Clear <strong>Submitted by</strong> and press Tab.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> "Submitter name is required" under it and in the error summary, before you press <strong>Submit</strong> again.</div></li>
            <li>Click <strong>Submit</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> the submit is blocked, and focus moves into <strong>Submitted by</strong>.</div></li>
            <li>Fill in <strong>Submitted by</strong> and click <strong>Submit</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> "Submitted" in the status line.</div></li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Submitted by</strong>: required.</li>
            <li><strong>Description</strong> (each expense line): required.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <HowItWorks>
        <p><code>FormidableValidator</code> attaches to the <code>EditContext</code> that the page's own <code>EditForm</code> passes down to its contents, rather than making a form of its own. Everything on the page that needs Formidable, from the error summary to the expense lines, sits inside the <code>FormidableValidator</code>.</p>
        <p><strong>Submitted by</strong> stays the plain <code>InputText</code> the form already had, so on its own it never tells the form it is on the page. The <code>FormidableFieldAnchor</code> beside it (a component that renders nothing) does that for it. For a submit to show a plain input's errors, that one line is all the input needs; its aria attributes and its focus take more, as the paragraphs below show. The expense lines use Formidable's own inputs.</p>
        <p>As you edit, a field you have changed shows its messages, anchor or not. A submit is different: it shows errors only for the fields the form counts as on the page. The anchor is what puts <strong>Submitted by</strong> among them.</p>
        <p>Whether an edit or a submit raises it, <strong>Submitted by</strong>'s message reaches the page through Blazor's own <code>ValidationMessage</code>, not through <code>FormidableFieldMessage</code> (the component that shows one field's message).</p>
        <p>The anchor does not carry the screen reader's half. A Formidable input writes its own aria attributes, and of those a plain <code>InputText</code> writes only <code>aria-invalid</code>, so the page writes the other two itself. <code>aria-required</code> says whether the rules a submit checks require the field, and it stands whatever the value does.</p>
        <p><code>aria-describedby</code> names the id the page also gives the <code>ValidationMessage</code>, but only while that component shows a message. It renders nothing when there is none, and naming an absent element describes nothing.</p>
        <p>Blazor's own <code>InputText</code> writes <code>aria-invalid</code> from the field's messages, so this page leaves that attribute to it.</p>
        <p>The page writes its two attributes from the form's state, so it has to re-render when that state changes. It subscribes to the validator's <code>Engine.StateChanged</code> event, the same subscription Formidable's own inputs make for themselves.</p>
        <p>Each line's <strong>Description</strong> is a Formidable input. It tells the form it is on the page exactly as it would under <code>FormidableForm</code> (the form component the other sample pages use, which renders its own <code>&lt;form&gt;</code>). It stops when its line leaves the list, which is what removing a line shows.</p>
        <p>The error summary lists the empty line's error and the one on <strong>Submitted by</strong> alike, because it shows what the inline messages show. As you edit, it lists a changed field's messages. After a submit, it lists the errors of the fields the form counts as on the page, and the anchor puts the plain input among them, just as a Formidable input puts itself there.</p>
        <p>Each line keeps its error by its object, not its place in the list, with the same <code>@@key</code> habit as the <a href="collections">Nested collections</a> page.</p>
        <p>The page's own submit handler calls <code>ValidateForSubmitAsync()</code> on the <code>FormidableValidator</code> itself, not on the validation state beneath it (its <code>Engine</code>). That brings the rest of a submit with it: a blocked submit moves focus to the first error, as it does under <code>FormidableForm</code>, under the same <code>FocusFirstErrorOnInvalidSubmit</code> switch.</p>
        <p><code>FormidableValidator</code> renders no <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> of its own (the <code>EditForm</code> renders this page's), and it never works out where the fields sit on the page; <code>FormidableForm</code> does. So the error summary lists errors in the order Formidable reports them, not down the page, and "first" means first in that order. A submit's entries keep close to the order the validator declares its rules.</p>
        <p>Focus finds a field by an element id Formidable derives from it. Formidable's own inputs carry that id; a plain input does not, so a focus aimed at <strong>Submitted by</strong> has nothing to land on until something supplies one. The anchor lets a submit show the field's errors; it does not supply the id.</p>
        <p><code>FocusFallback</code> (a callback a focus move calls when it misses) is for exactly that. One callback here, wired to both the validator and the error summary, renders the id and returns <code>true</code>, and the move tries once more. It has a single miss to recover: the id stays rendered, so every later focus on the field lands first time. The <a href="vanilla">Vanilla interop</a> page instead gives its native input the id from the start.</p>
        <p><code>FormidableValidator</code> also offers <code>NotifyFieldSetChanged()</code>, for code that reads its <code>Engine</code> straight after changing the list, before the validator has noticed the change on its own. This page never calls it: removing a line is enough on its own.</p>
    </HowItWorks>
    <Demo>
        <EditForm Model="_report" OnSubmit="HandleSubmit" id="@GateId" tabindex="-1">
            @* Everything that needs the cascaded FormidableFormContext — FormidableSummary and the row's
               own FormidableInputText/FormidableFieldMessage — has to be FormidableValidator's own
               ChildContent, not its sibling: with no ChildContent, FormidableValidator renders nothing
               at all, cascade included, rather than an empty wrapper. Context= names the typed
               fragment's parameter, because EditForm's own child content already claims the implicit
               "context" and the Razor compiler refuses to nest two claims on one name. What collides is
               the claim, not any use of it: the rename is owed even though nothing below reads the
               parameter, as nothing here does. *@
            <FormidableValidator TModel="ExpenseReport" @ref="_validator" FocusFallback="MakeSubmitterReachableAsync" Context="formidable">
                <div class="summary-slot">
                    <FormidableSummary FocusFallback="MakeSubmitterReachableAsync" />
                </div>

                <div class="field">
                    <label>Submitted by <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _report.SubmitterName" /> <InputText @bind-Value="_report.SubmitterName" id="@_submitterElementId" aria-describedby="@SubmitterAriaDescribedBy" aria-required="@SubmitterAriaRequired" /></label>
                    <ValidationMessage For="() => _report.SubmitterName" id="@SubmitterMessagesId" />
                    <FormidableFieldAnchor For="() => _report.SubmitterName" />
                </div>

                <ul class="member-list">
                    @foreach (var line in _report.Lines)
                    {
                        <li class="field" @key="line">
                            <label>Description <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => line.Description" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="line.Description" /></label>
                            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => line.Description" />
                            <div class="actions">
                                <button type="button" @onclick="() => RemoveLine(line)">Remove</button>
                            </div>
                        </li>
                    }
                </ul>

                <div class="actions">
                    <button type="button" @onclick="AddLine">Add expense</button>
                    <button type="submit">Submit</button>
                </div>
            </FormidableValidator>
        </EditForm>

        <p role="status">@_status</p>
    </Demo>
</TeachingPanel>
